<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mers El Kébir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mers el-Kébir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mers El Kébir is a port town on the Algerian coast a few kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a>, with one of the best natural harbours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> held it from 1505, with Oran, until 1708 and again from 1732 to 1792. Under French rule it became a naval base, where on 3 July 1940 the British fleet attacked French warships to keep them from German control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mers-el-kebir.jpg" alt="Mers El Kébir" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bachounda · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MOts_Murdjajou_surplombant_Oran_(13).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name is Arabic, al-Marsā al-Kabīr, meaning the Great Harbor. The port was originally Phoenician, and under the Roman presence it was called Portus Divinus. In the 12th century it became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> naval arsenal, in the 13th century it fell under the rulers of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, and around 1492 it became a centre of pirate activity. Ottoman Turks, Portuguese and Spanish fought over it. The Portuguese were defeated there by Abu Abdallah IV in 1501, and the Spanish by Abu Abd Allah V in 1507.</p><p>The Spanish, who called it Mazalquivir, captured it in 1505 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jimenez-de-cisneros">Cardinal Cisneros</a> and used it as a base to take neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> in 1509. They were driven out in 1708 by Bey Mustapha Ben Youssef. Their return in 1732 followed the victory of the armada of the Duke of Montemar at the Battle of Aïn-el-Turk. In 1792 King Carlos IV sold both cities to the Dey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> after a siege by the Dey's forces. The French occupied the port in 1830 and renamed it Saint André de Mers-el-Kébir, improving it in 1868 with a lighthouse that was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1940 a French fleet took shelter in the port. On 3 July 1940 Force H of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, led by Vice-admiral Sir James Somerville, attacked it after the French commanders refused all of Somerville's demands, which were designed to ensure that the ships would not join forces with Nazi Germany, as Vichy France had. Force H destroyed or damaged several French warships. The attack led Vichy France to sever relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and it showed the world that the British would fight on against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a>.</p><p>After the war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> used its naval base at Mers-el-Kébir to assist in atomic tests, and developed the base substantially in 1953. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evian-accords">Évian Accords</a> of 18 March 1962, which recognised Algerian independence, allowed France to keep the base for 15 years, but France withdrew five years later, in 1967. The Algerian navy uses the base.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mers El Kébir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the fall of Granada, Spain seized a chain of North African ports, among them <b>Mers el-Kébir</b> in 1505, Oran in 1509 and Bejaia in 1510, and around 1510 it built a fort on the Peñón, an islet commanding the harbour of Algiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 3 July 1940 the Royal Navy attacked a French fleet at <b>Mers El Kébir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vichy-france">Vichy France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After taking <b>Mers el-Kébir</b> in 1505, Castile captured Oran in 1509 in an expedition financed and led by Cardinal Cisneros, with Pedro Navarro commanding the troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-oran">Spanish Oran</a></li></ul></section>
